The chapter is mainly about viewing, searching, filtering, sorting and redirecting text in Linux.

1. cat — display text

cat displays the contents of a file.

cat file.txt

It can also create a file:

cat > new.txt

Then type your text and press Ctrl+D to finish.

You can combine files:

cat file1.txt file2.txt

2. less — view large files
less file.txt

Important keys:

Key	What it does
Space	Move forward one screen
b	Move backward one screen
Enter	Move forward one line
q	Quit
h	Help
/word	Search forward
?word	Search backward
n	Next search match
N	Previous search match

Easy way to remember:
less = read a file one screen at a time.

3. head and tail

head shows the beginning of a file.

head file.txt

By default, it shows 10 lines.

Show 3 lines:

head -n 3 file.txt

tail shows the end of a file.

tail file.txt

Show the last 5 lines:

tail -n 5 file.txt

A particularly useful command:

tail -f /var/log/somefile.log

-f means follow the file, so you can watch new information being added.

This is very useful for troubleshooting and monitoring logs.

4. Pipes |

This is one of the most important concepts in the chapter.

The pipe takes the output of one command and sends it as input to another command.

command1 | command2

Example:

ls /etc | head

ls produces the information → | passes it → head processes it.

You can have multiple pipes:

ls /etc/ssh | nl | tail -5

This means:

ls
 ↓
nl
 ↓
tail
 ↓
screen

The order matters.

For example:

ls /etc/ssh | nl | tail -5

numbers everything first and then takes the last five.

But:

ls /etc/ssh | tail -5 | nl

takes the last five first and then numbers those five from 1–5.

Key exam idea

A pipe sends STDOUT from one command to STDIN of another command.

5. Standard streams

Stream	Number	Meaning
STDIN	0	Input
STDOUT	1	Normal output
STDERR	2	Error output

An easy way to remember:

0 = Input
1 = Normal output
2 = Error

By default, they normally use the terminal.

6. Output redirection >

The > symbol sends STDOUT into a file.

echo "Hello" > example.txt

Instead of appearing on the screen, Hello goes into example.txt.

> overwrites the existing file.

For example:

echo "First" > example.txt
echo "Second" > example.txt

The file will contain:

Second

The first line is gone.

7. Append >>

>> adds information to the end of a file.

echo "First" > example.txt
echo "Second" >> example.txt

The file becomes:

First
Second
Remember
>   = overwrite
>>  = append

This is an important exam point.

8. Redirect errors with 2>

Remember:

STDERR = 2

So:

ls /fake 2> error.txt

sends the error message into error.txt.

You can then see it:

cat error.txt
Remember
>    = STDOUT
2>   = STDERR
9. Redirect STDOUT and STDERR together

You can send both to the same file:

command &> all.txt

For example:

ls /fake /etc/ppp &> all.txt

Both normal output and errors go into all.txt.

You can also send them to different files:

command > output.txt 2> error.txt

So:

STDOUT → output.txt
STDERR → error.txt
10. STDIN redirection <

The < symbol tells a command to take its input from a file instead of the keyboard.

Example:

tr 'a-z' 'A-Z' < example.txt

The file becomes the input to tr.

You can also redirect the output:

tr 'a-z' 'A-Z' < example.txt > newexample.txt

Think:

example.txt
     ↓
   STDIN
     ↓
    tr
     ↓
   STDOUT
     ↓
newexample.txt
11. sort

sort sorts lines alphabetically by default.

sort file.txt

Useful options:

Option	Meaning
-r	Reverse order
-n	Numeric sorting
-t	Specify delimiter
-k	Specify field to sort

For example:

sort -t: -n -k3 mypasswd

Means:

-t: → fields are separated by :
-k3 → sort using field 3
-n → sort numerically
Important

Fields start at 1, not 0.

12. wc

wc = word count

It can count:

lines
words
bytes
wc file.txt

You will normally see:

lines   words   bytes   filename

Useful options:

wc -l file.txt

Count lines.

wc -w file.txt

Count words.

wc -c file.txt

Count bytes.

A very useful combination:

ls /etc | wc -l

This counts the lines produced by ls.

13. grep — search/filter text

This is very important.

grep searches for a pattern.

grep bash /etc/passwd

This displays lines containing bash.

Important grep options
Command	Meaning
grep word file	Search for word
grep -c word file	Count matching lines
grep -n word file	Show line numbers
grep -v word file	Show lines that DON'T match
grep -i word file	Ignore case
grep -w word file	Match whole word

For example:

grep -c bash /etc/passwd

means:

Count the number of lines containing bash.

14. Regular expressions

Regular expressions, or regex, allow you to search for patterns rather than just exact words.

The main ones you need to know are:

Regex	Meaning
.	Any single character
[abc]	One character from a, b or c
[a-z]	One character from a–z
[^0-9]	A character that is NOT 0–9
*	Zero or more occurrences
^	Beginning of line
$	End of line
15. The period .

. means any single character.

For example:

grep 'r..f' red.txt

The pattern means:

r
any character
any character
f

So:

reef
roof

match.

16. Brackets [ ]

Brackets allow you to specify possible characters.

[abc]

means:

match one character that is either a, b, or c.

You can use a range:

[0-9]

means any digit from 0 to 9.

Or:

[a-z]

means any lowercase letter.

Negation
[^0-9]

means:

match a character that is not a number.


17. Asterisk *

* means zero or more occurrences of the previous character/pattern.

For example:

re*d

can match:

rd
red
reed
reeed

because the e can occur zero or more times.

Important

* applies to what comes immediately before it.

18. Anchors ^ and $

These are very important.

^ = beginning
grep '^root' /etc/passwd

Means:

Find lines that start with root.

$ = end
grep 'r$' alpha-first.txt

Means:

Find lines that end with r.

Think:

^ = start
$ = end
19. Escaping special characters

Sometimes you want to search for an actual special character.

For example, * normally has a special regex meaning.

To search for a literal *, use:

\*

Example:

grep 're\*' newhome.txt

The backslash \ tells grep:

Treat this special character literally.

20. Extended Regular Expressions

Use:

grep -E

Important extended regex symbols:

Symbol	Meaning
?	Zero or one
+	One or more
`	`

Example:

grep -E 'colou?r' spelling.txt

Matches:

color
colour

because u is optional.

Another:

grep -E 'e+' red.txt

means:

one or more e characters.

And:

grep -E 'gray|grey' spelling.txt

means:

Match gray OR grey.


